      "latency_ms": 22888.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1154,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more electronegative (it pulls electrons toward itself) than hydrogen, the molecule has a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a slight negative charge on the oxygen side. This polarity allows water to stick to itself (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion), and it is the reason water is such an effective solvent.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, a liquid, and a gas at temperatures commonly found on the planet:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules form a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid (Water):** This is the state we are most familiar with. It flows and takes the shape of its container.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast that they escape into the air as a gas.\n\n### 3. Key Properties\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is vital for biological processes, as it allows nutrients to be transported through the body and plants.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water has a strong \"skin\" on its surface, which allows insects to walk on it and for water to form perfect spherical droplets.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the Earth's climate (oceans absorb heat during the day and release it at night) and keeps human bodies from overheating.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is essential for life as we know it. It is the primary component of all living things:\n*   **Cellular Function:** It makes up about 60% of the human body and is necessary for chemical reactions to occur.\n*   **Transport:** It moves nutrients and waste products in and out of cells.\n*   **Temperature Regulation:** Through sweating and respiration, water helps maintain a stable body temperature.\n\nIn short, water is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and essential liquid that acts as the fundamental building block of life on Earth."
